WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.360
Is the King of the Monsters also the king of&nbsp;
the box office? 4 billion dollars for Lucasfilm;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.880
was it worth it? Was Wolverine strong enough&nbsp;
to support the entire X-Men movie franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.240
on his furry back? These movies brought back&nbsp;
long dormant franchises to a planet near you.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.680
"Star Trek" is one of the most influential&nbsp;
and long-running media franchises of all time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.960
It's easy to forget that the original "Star Trek"&nbsp;
television series was unceremoniously canceled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.080
after its third season in 1969. "Star&nbsp;
Trek" gained cultural momentum in reruns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.200
during the 1970s, though, leading to a&nbsp;
brief revival as an animated series and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.800
a planned return to live-action&nbsp;
television called "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:48.000
Phase II." After the box success of "Star Wars"&nbsp;
in 1977, Paramount changed course and relaunched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.520
"Star Trek" on the big screen, resulting&nbsp;
in 1979's "Star Trek: The Motion Picture."

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.440
"The Motion Picture" was a financial success but a&nbsp;
critical failure whose plodding pace earned it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.720
fan nickname "The Slow-Motion Picture." Paramount&nbsp;
was willing to try making another "Star Trek"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.920
film, but only if it could be done on a much&nbsp;
smaller budget. But despite being made on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.520
cheap by unproven director Nicholas Meyer, "Star&nbsp;
Trek II: The Wrath of Khan" made an immediate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.200
cultural impact, becoming a legitimate mainstream&nbsp;
hit and winning back eight times its budget.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.960
"The Wrath of Khan" launched a new,&nbsp;
prosperous era in which "Star Trek"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.600
thrived both on the big screen and back&nbsp;
on television for another two decades.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.301
"KHAAAAAAAN!"

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.880
Following the success of "Wrath&nbsp;
of Khan," "Star Trek" became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.080
pop culture brand that was&nbsp;
everywhere, spanning movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.040
television, and beyond. The original cast&nbsp;
continued to star in feature films throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.800
the '80s before hanging it up in "Star&nbsp;
Trek VI: The Undiscovered Country" in 1991.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.280
Meanwhile, "Trek" was undergoing a renaissance&nbsp;
on television, with a string of TV spin-offs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.040
that ran for 18 consecutive years and&nbsp;
a cumulative 624 episodes. "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.760
The Next Generation" was a big enough hit to&nbsp;
warrant its own leap to the big screen, which kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.680
the film series alive until the critical and box&nbsp;
office failure of "Star Trek: Nemesis" in 2002.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.160
When "Trek's" television run likewise came to&nbsp;
an end with the cancellation of "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.840
Enterprise" in 2005, the franchise went into&nbsp;
stasis for the first time since the mid-1970s.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.800
Paramount wouldn't let "Trek" rest for too long.&nbsp;
In 2007, J.J. Abrams was officially attached to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.560
direct a new part-sequel, part-prequel,&nbsp;
part-reboot simply titled "Star Trek."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.040
This radical reimagining of the original&nbsp;
source material reignited mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.360
interest in the franchise, making it one&nbsp;
of the highest-grossing films of 2009.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.720
Since then, "Trek" has had two more feature&nbsp;
film sequels and yet another television revival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.600
with producer Alex Kurtzman now at the helm&nbsp;
of the franchise's five new streaming series.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.360
Beginning with its first installment in 2000,&nbsp;
the "X-Men" franchise was at the forefront of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.480
the superhero movie boom. Each chapter of the&nbsp;
original "X-Men" trilogy outgrossed the last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.520
raking in a cumulative billion dollars&nbsp;
in worldwide box office receipts.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.840
With the original cast's story concluded,&nbsp;
20th Century Fox sought to expand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.080
franchise with a variety of new spin-offs,&nbsp;
beginning with "X-Men Origins: Wolverine"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.920
in 2009. "Wolverine" introduced the characters&nbsp;
of Gambit and Deadpool to the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.560
and they were each eyed for their own spin-offs.&nbsp;
Additionally, two more prequels were planned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.000
one centered around Magneto and another on&nbsp;
Charles Xavier's original class of X-Men.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.920
But after the messy release and critical failure&nbsp;
of "X-Men Origins: Wolverine," 20th Century Fox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.200
put the rest of their "X-Men" slate on hold,&nbsp;
focusing instead on a single project: the prequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.480
film "X-Men: First Class," which featured&nbsp;
an entirely new cast and a period setting.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.200
"First Class" didn't immediately reverse the&nbsp;
franchise's box office fortunes, earning about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.400
$10 million less than "X-Men Origins: Wolverine"&nbsp;
on a similar budget, but it was better received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.120
by fans and critics, putting the franchise back&nbsp;
in the right direction. Its immediate sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:10.720
"X-Men: Days of Future Past," combined the&nbsp;
new and original X-Men film casts and became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.640
a massive box office hit. Incidentally, "Days&nbsp;
of Future Past" also erased the poorly received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.600
"Last Stand" and "Origins" from continuity&nbsp;
by way of time travel. Neat trick, huh?

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:28.560
1968's "Planet of the Apes" is one of the sci-fi&nbsp;
genre's best films, as well as the launchpad of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.920
its first long-running sci-fi film franchises.&nbsp;
The original "Planet of the Apes" spawned four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:40.480
direct sequels in rapid succession between 1970&nbsp;
and 1973. And when the series fizzled out on film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.360
the franchise moved to television — first as a&nbsp;
live-action series, then in animation — but both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.360
efforts were short-lived. With&nbsp;
the cartoon's cancelation in 1975,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.160
"Planet of the Apes" disappeared from both&nbsp;
film and television for a quarter-century.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.960
A big-budget, Tim Burton-helmed reimagining&nbsp;
of "Planet of the Apes" was released in 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:04.000
which turned a profit but was such a critical&nbsp;
disaster that the franchise was immediately put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.600
back on the shelf. The next reboot attempt came&nbsp;
a decade later with 2011's prequel "Rise of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.160
Planet of the Apes." It gave new life to the&nbsp;
franchise, replacing the previous generations'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.840
make-up effects with remarkably expressive CGI&nbsp;
apes performed via motion capture by the likes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.200
of Andy Serkis. "Rise" spawned a successful&nbsp;
new "Apes" trilogy whose middle chapter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.840
"Dawn of the Planet of the Apes," was a&nbsp;
critical darling as well as a smash hit.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.520
Unsurprisingly, another reboot of the&nbsp;
franchise is already in the works.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.960
"Get your stinking paws off&nbsp;
me, you damn dirty ape!”

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.720
Ridley Scott's 1979 sci-fi/horror mashup&nbsp;
"Alien" is a classic in both genres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.600
so it's remarkable that anyone was able to&nbsp;
create even one sequel of comparable quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.240
in the first place. James Cameron's&nbsp;
1986 follow-up "Aliens" triumphed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:59.920
continuing the journey of original&nbsp;
film protagonist Ellen Ripley with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.720
a totally different approach and tone,&nbsp;
but this trick proved difficult to repeat.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:10.400
1992's "Alien 3" distanced itself from the&nbsp;
action-oriented tone of "Aliens" and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.040
poorly received, while 1997's "Alien:&nbsp;
Resurrection" seemed to overcorrect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.760
becoming a bizarre comic book-y affair that&nbsp;
grossed poorly in North America. The brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.560
was kept alive for a time by crossing over with&nbsp;
the similarly struggling "Predator" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.760
but the "Alien vs. Predator" film series&nbsp;
likewise fell apart after two entries.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.760
It would take Ridley Scott himself to lend&nbsp;
credibility back to the universe that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:39.440
helped create. His 2012 film "Prometheus" is&nbsp;
set in the same universe as "Alien," but follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.320
another set of characters on a journey that's&nbsp;
only tangentially connected to the events of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.680
the original film. More importantly, it shares&nbsp;
"Alien's" moody, unsettling tone and gruesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.400
body horror, and added even loftier&nbsp;
philosophical and ontological themes.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.720
While reviews were mixed, "Prometheus" became&nbsp;
the highest-grossing film in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.480
leading to sequel "Alien: Covenant,"&nbsp;
which also serves as a more direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.480
prequel to the original film. With&nbsp;
interest in "Alien" fully revitalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.160
an additional film and a spin-off&nbsp;
television series are forthcoming.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.080
Godzilla is one of cinema's most prolific&nbsp;
and iconic monsters, having starred in 36&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:22.320
films since 1954. His longevity as a movie icon&nbsp;
has been assured by his occasionally going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:30.320
hibernation, as he did in 1975, 1995, and finally&nbsp;
in 2004. On the occasion of the original film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:35.360
50th anniversary, Japanese studio Toho released&nbsp;
"Godzilla: Final Wars," which brought an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.800
end to the "Millennium era" of Godzilla movies and&nbsp;
put the famous kaiju to rest for the third time.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:47.600
A decade later, Gareth Edwards' 2014 American&nbsp;
"Godzilla" was a critical and box office success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.880
Not only did the 2014 "Godzilla" lead to the&nbsp;
expansion of Legendary's "Monsterverse," (crossing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.080
Godzilla over with King Kong for the first time&nbsp;
in half a century), but also prompted Toho to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:03.520
revive Godzilla in Japan. "Godzilla Resurgence"&nbsp;
was not only a hit at the Japanese box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:09.120
in 2016 but also the recipient of the Japan&nbsp;
Academy Film Prize for Picture of the Year.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.280
The original 1979 "Mad Max" is a small&nbsp;
miracle of filmmaking. The debut feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.920
from writer/director George Miller was produced&nbsp;
for less than half a million dollars and became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:25.520
such a hit that it held the world record for the&nbsp;
most profitable film in terms of cost vs. gross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:30.880
until it was toppled by "The Blair Witch&nbsp;
Project" in 1999. Its two sequels, "Mad Max&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:37.120
2" (aka "The Road Warrior") and "Mad Max Beyond&nbsp;
Thunderdome" were increasingly expensive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.560
outlandish, but also won increasing financial&nbsp;
returns. "Beyond Thunderdome" was a hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.720
but was considered a slight step down&nbsp;
in quality from its predecessors.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.800
After directing three "Mad Max" films&nbsp;
in six years, Miller was certain that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:57.760
he was finished with the series in 1985. But&nbsp;
inspiration for "Fury Road" struck hard in 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.800
leading to a torturously long development&nbsp;
process. The film was almost made in 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:09.280
then again in 2010, before cameras finally&nbsp;
began rolling in 2012. By the time "Mad Max:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:14.480
Fury Road" hit theaters in 2015, the&nbsp;
series had been dormant for 30 years.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:19.440
The wait was worth it — "Fury Road" was an&nbsp;
unqualified critical and commercial success that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.480
earned 10 Academy Award nominations, including&nbsp;
Best Picture. Audiences clamored for more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.360
and a franchise was reborn. Two more "Mad Max"&nbsp;
films are currently in development, one featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:34.880
series lead Max and one expanding on the origins&nbsp;
of the breakout character of “Fury Road”- Furiosa.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.000
There should probably never have been a sequel&nbsp;
to "Jurassic Park" in the first place — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.720
moral of the original film is that a theme park&nbsp;
full of sharp-toothed, ravenous, prehistoric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.640
monsters is a bad idea that should never be tried&nbsp;
again. But try they have, over and over again.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.960
After the 1993 original came the underwhelming&nbsp;
1997 sequel "The Lost World: Jurassic Park."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:01.120
If Steven Spielberg himself couldn't make&nbsp;
a successful sequel to his own classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:06.160
what hope could anyone else have? 2001's&nbsp;
"Jurassic Park III" was even less successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:09.760
but plans for a "Jurassic Park IV"&nbsp;
continued to roll on nevertheless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:12.640
with a decade-long marathon of rejected scripts.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:19.200
Finally, in 2015, Universal and director Colin&nbsp;
Trevorrow released their long-awaited, big-budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.560
attempt to clone their cash cow, "Jurassic World."&nbsp;
Sporting an entirely new set of characters but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:29.840
deeply rooted in nostalgia for the original,&nbsp;
"Jurassic World" was a colossal box office hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:35.600
raking in over $1.6 billion worldwide and&nbsp;
spawning a pair of sequels of its own.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.800
Like their genetically reconstituted stars, the&nbsp;
"Jurassic World" films really shouldn't exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:44.000
but there's no denying that the&nbsp;
franchise is alive and well.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.880
From the moment Disney purchased&nbsp;
Lucasfilm for $4 billion in 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:52.400
it was a sure thing that "Star Wars"&nbsp;
would be coming back to the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.720
and nearly as certain that their first&nbsp;
movie out of the gate would be a smash hit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:02.000
Just as in the case of 1999's "The Phantom&nbsp;
Menace," anticipation and speculation alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:06.960
would have brought filmgoers out in droves to&nbsp;
see if the old "Star Wars" magic had come back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:12.080
no matter how disappointed they may have been in&nbsp;
George Lucas' prequel trilogy a decade earlier.

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:16.640
What's remarkable about "Star Wars: Episode VII —&nbsp;
The Force Awakens," though, is the sheer magnitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:23.680
of its success. It grossed over $936 million in&nbsp;
the U.S. alone and over $2 billion worldwide. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:29.440
also got the warmest reception from critics of any&nbsp;
"Star Wars" film since "The Empire Strikes Back."

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.720
Fans may have mixed feelings as to the overall&nbsp;
quality of the post-buyout "Star Wars" brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:39.200
but "The Force Awakens" announced loud and&nbsp;
clear that "Star Wars" was back in a big way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.840
It's unlikely to be retired again any time soon.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.600
The Marvel Cinematic Universe, the most&nbsp;
financially successful film franchise of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.960
is essentially one big franchise made of many&nbsp;
smaller ones. Almost every one of the 23 films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:58.960
in its ambitious "Infinity Saga" won its opening&nbsp;
weekend, turned a profit, and spawned a sequel.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:02.560
Sure, all the movies were hits, but&nbsp;
answer us this question — after "Thor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:07.440
The Dark World," was anybody out there&nbsp;
really clamoring for more solo Thor movies?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.560
"The Dark World" has the lowest Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;
score in the "Infinity Saga," and the first "Thor"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:17.760
doesn't score much higher. With Chris Hemsworth's&nbsp;
original Marvel contract expiring with "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:22.320
Endgame," who's to say there'd have been enough&nbsp;
heat behind the character to renew any further?

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.760
Director Taika Waititi, that's who! Waititi&nbsp;
took this ailing corner of the MCU for a hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:31.520
left turn with "Thor: Ragnarok," giving&nbsp;
it a new sense of humor and a new cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:36.400
of supporting characters. "Ragnarok" outgrossed&nbsp;
"The Dark World" globally by over $200 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:43.200
and rests near the top of many lists ranking the&nbsp;
MCU's best films. Its success prompted rewrites of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.520
"Avengers: Infinity War," and the more comedic&nbsp;
version of the character is set to return for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:52.160
another "Thor" film with Waititi at the helm.&nbsp;
Hemsworth hopes to continue to play Thor for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:57.120
years to come, something that almost certainly&nbsp;
wouldn't have happened if not for "Ragnarok."

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.600
"I thought you were gone forever!&nbsp;
I'm glad to see you back!"

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:03.120
"You're ruining my disguise!"

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:06.000
Check out one of our newest&nbsp;
videos right here! Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.760
even more Looper videos about your&nbsp;
favorite sci-fi franchises are coming soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:13.401
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit&nbsp;
the bell so you don't miss a single one.

